EXPERIMENT 1: Analyze sample code snippets to identify basic security flaws

AIM: Study C / Python code snippets to identify common vulnerabilities like buffer overflow, insecure
input handling, or hard coded credentials. Tools like static analyzers or manual code walkthroughs
can be used.

Project Structure:
├── bufferoverflow/          # Buffer overflow vulnerabilities (C)
├── formatstringmisuse/      # Format string vulnerabilities
├── hardcodedcredentials/    # Hardcoded sensitive information
├── unsafeoscommands/        # OS command injection vulnerabilities

Vulnerabilities Covered:
(1) Buffer Overflow
A buffer overflow is a condition where a program writes data beyond the boundaries of allocated memory, potentially leading to memory corruption, crashes, or arbitrary code execution.

(2) Format String Misuse
Format string misuse occurs when untrusted input is used as the format string parameter in formatted output functions, which may allow an attacker to read or write memory.

(3) Hardcoded Credentials
Hardcoded credentials refer to authentication information such as usernames or passwords that are embedded directly in source code, making them susceptible to unauthorized access if the code is exposed.

(4) Unsafe OS Commands
Unsafe OS command usage occurs when unvalidated or untrusted input is passed to system command execution functions, potentially allowing attackers to execute arbitrary commands on the host system.


⚠️ Disclaimer

This project is intended for educational purposes only. The vulnerabilities demonstrated are used to understand and improve security practices.
